 the chip, opening out with the radio rings, dropping away at the blank field card and returning softly for the goal.\",\"perspective\":\"Designed illustration sound for rain, water, steel, paper and a quiet hub room. Never recorded at a UT San Antonio site, a real station or a real alert.\",\"voice_arc\":\"Plain and close over the flooded yard, quicker through the station and its marks, a lift on the chip turn, steady through the radio ranges, then slower and level on the validation figure and the close.\"},\"viewer_question\":\"Can a flood station judge rising street water with no power line and no cell tower?\",\"visible_answer\":\"The chip lights a local judgment, a radio pulse reaches a hub map, and the closing map shows no installed pin.\"},\"credits\":\"TEXAS AI DOCKET\\nSOURCES\\nUT San Antonio Today, September 8th\\nILLUSTRATION\\nOriginal authored diagrams\\nMAP\\nCounty marker is editorial\\n\",\"credits_s\":5,\"daily_production\":true,\"date\":\"2026-10-15\",\"derived_from\":\"scratch\",\"divergence_note\":\"A opens wide in a flooded yard with no station and pushes in on the orange ankle mark, tilts up the prototype, climbs its column, enters its chip, follows the radio at street level into open country, reads the hub desk before its map, and closes at the empty curb where a dashed station outline draws itself. B opens on a disclosed design detail of the lowest lens going under, match cuts on ankle height to the same kind of yard, cranes down the prototype, holds the panel close through the tower beat, sees the readings arrive through the housing window from the street, reads the radio from above, the hub map before the desk, both cards side by side, and closes on the same map close up. Both perform every event in clause order; they differ in opening, staging, framing, camera and which view carries each clause.\",\"documentary\":{\"closing_answer\":\"The design says yes. The proof so far is validation accuracy, and no installed place is named.\",\"hero_i