10-15/FloodStationHero.tsx\",\"original_source\":\"artwork-originals/authored-6555a67c-7cdb-41f0-a2ef-f76c9518c055.tsx\",\"request_id\":\"flood-station\",\"request_sha256\":\"22fdead6fce9a74ebd117d48dd6c116dacaab1e5f0ecca459d51a2c28a6f333b\",\"sha256\":\"f3dbf42717bbea88758c825d05cfeda588ebbe2ddbfcf493e3c5ddd86cc5461c\",\"source_claims_sha256\":\"49b2379c63534c1685c5fcd480bbf0aa8cadee104811bfe9abb29cbb14f6438e\",\"tool\":\"original authored source\"},{\"charge\":{\"$v\":\"18\"},\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T03:18:15.476762+00:00\",\"creation_id\":\"authored-fd230d9b-a832-442a-9c5b-ed5932403b81\",\"file\":\"video-engine/src/modern/episodes/2026-10-15/StreetHubSupport.tsx\",\"original_source\":\"artwork-originals/authored-fd230d9b-a832-442a-9c5b-ed5932403b81.tsx\",\"request_id\":\"street-hub-support\",\"request_sha256\":\"3e86669f7eff9e8dc1d1dc73a3d8d4c74ac443fc02936277568af2d832f6fcce\",\"sha256\":\"916e12f7be70b88b289e29b42c6e4961fe22dfd0e70027943339dc61edf3de91\",\"source_claims_sha256\":\"49b2379c63534c1685c5fcd480bbf0aa8cadee104811bfe9abb29cbb14f6438e\",\"tool\":\"original authored source\"}],\"requests\":[{\"action_uses\":[{\"action_id\":\"charge-battery\",\"event_id\":\"s2-event-2\",\"scene_id\":\"s2\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s2-panel\",\"s2-battery\"],\"view\":\"panel-charge\"},{\"action_id\":\"cross-marks\",\"event_id\":\"s3-event-2\",\"scene_id\":\"s3\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s3-marks\",\"s3-water\"],\"view\":\"water-marks\"},{\"action_id\":\"judge-locally\",\"event_id\":\"s4-event-2\",\"scene_id\":\"s4\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s4-chip\",\"s4-risk\"],\"view\":\"chip-glow\"}],\"export\":\"FloodStationHero\",\"file\":\"video-engine/src/modern/episodes/2026-10-15/FloodStationHero.tsx\",\"id\":\"flood-station\",\"prompt\":\"Author an original finished React and SVG object in the current Remotion engine. A weathered galvanized steel post set beside a San Antonio street with a lopsided, slightly tilted instrument housing, a small dark blue solar panel with a worn corner, a short whip antenna, a battery bar window, and a column of four optical water-level marks mounted at four different heights, each with a small lens and a lamp. A cutaway view shows a small chip with thin traces and one amber risk lamp, with no logos and no real hardware brand. Late afternoon storm light from the upper left, cool sky fill, a long soft shadow, a rust drip and a dent, nothing symmetric. A disclosed illustration of the described design, never an installed unit.\",\"purpose\":\"Carry one solar flood station through the film so the viewer follows its sun-charged battery, its four optical marks lighting as the water crosses them, the chip that lights an amber risk lamp on the spot and the antenna that sends the radio pulse, with every state it reaches kept for the rest of the film.\",\"role\":\"hero\",\"scene_ids\":[\"s1\",\"s2\",\"s3\",\"s4\",\"s5\",\"s6\",\"s7\",\"s8\",\"s9\"],\"source_limit\":\"Disclosed illustration of the design the University of Texas at San Antonio describes. Not an installed station, a real street, a measured water level or a real result.\"},{\"action_uses\":[{\"action_id\":\"water-rises\",\"event_id\":\"s1-event-1\",\"scene_id\":\"s1\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s1-water\",\"s1-post\"],\"view\":\"street-yard\"},{\"action_id\":\"carry-signal\",\"event_id\":\"s5-event-2\",\"scene_id\":\"s5\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s5-ring\",\"s5-halfmile\"],\"view\":\"radio-rings\"},{\"action_id\":\"spread-map\",\"event_id\":\"s6-event-2\",\"scene_id\":\"s6\",\"subject_ids\":[\"s6-paths\",\"s6-dashboard\"],\"view\":\"flood-paths\"}],\"export\":\"StreetHubSupport\",\"file\":\"video-engine/src/modern/episodes/2026-10-15/StreetHubSupport.tsx\",\"id\":\"street-hub-support\",\"prompt\":\"Author an original finished React and SVG support group in the current Remotion engine. A wet San Antonio residential street with a chain link fence post, a limestone curb, a live oak edge and a porch rail with a worn phone, brown flood water with a hand drawn edge, a small hub building with a rooftop mast, a hub desk with a monitor and a sleeved forearm, a flat dashboard map of streets with shaded flood paths and outlined risk zones, an empty map card with no pin, and thin radio rings with a half-mile tag and a five-mile tag. Maintained but worn, nothing symmetric, warm limestone and storm gray palette, no real brand marks. A disclosed illustration.\",\"purpose\":\"Give the station a believable world: the San Antonio street and yard where water rises, the porch phone, the radio rings, the hub building, the hub dashboard map and the empty map.\",\"role\":\"support\",\"scene_ids\":[\"s1\",\"s2\",\"s3\",\"s4\",\"s5\",\"s6\",\"s7\",\"s8\",\"s9\"],\"source_limit\":\"Disclosed illustration. Not a real San Antonio address, hub, dashboard, responder or deployment. The university names no installation site.\"}],\"runtime\":\"claude-sonnet-v1\",\"version\":\"authored-story-art-v1\"},\"story_contract\":{\"action\":\"Built a prototype flood station that charges itself from light and runs its AI model on a small chip.\",\"actor\":\"University of Texas at San Antonio researchers led by Chen Pan\",\"affected_people\":\"Emergency responders and residents of street-level flood-prone blocks. No individual is named in any source.\",\"change\":\"A flood gauge that needs grid power and a network becomes a self-powered station that judges flood risk on its own chip and signals a hub by radio.\",\"closing_answer\":\"The design says yes. The proof so far is validation accuracy, and no installed place is named.\",\"consequence\":\"A hub dashboard with predictive maps of how flooding could spread, for emergency responders, per the university.\",\"director_identity\":\"claude-sonnet-5-5 director, this session\",\"opening_question\":\"Can a flood station judge rising street water with no power line and no cell tower?\",\"scenes\":[{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"22\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c12\"],\"role\":\"action\",\"scene_id\":\"s1\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"62\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c1\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c14\"],\"role\":\"action\",\"scene_id\":\"s2\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"11\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c2\"],\"role\":\"mechanism\",\"scene_id\":\"s3\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"54\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c3\",\"c18\"],\"role\":\"mechanism\",\"scene_id\":\"s4\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"87\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c6\"],\"role\":\"mechanism\",\"scene_id\":\"s5\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"66\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c7\",\"c16\",\"c17\",\"c20\"],\"role\":\"consequence\",\"scene_id\":\"s6\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"51\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c4\",\"c18\"],\"role\":\"limit\",\"scene_id\":\"s7\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"74\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c1\",\"c4\",\"c10\"],\"role\":\"limit\",\"scene_id\":\"s8\"},{\"advances\":{\"$v\":\"26\"},\"claim_ids\":[\"c9\",\"c10\"],\"role\":\"answer\",\"scene_id\":\"s9\"}],\"source_limit\":{\"$v\":\"43\"},\"transitions\":[{\"because\":\"The yard the warning reached late leads to the station the researchers built.\",\"from\":\"s1\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s2\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The same brown flood water carries from the yard to the prototype standing at a curb.\"},{\"because\":\"The same station is climbed from its panel and battery down to the marks that watch the water.\",\"from\":\"s2\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s3\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The same post and its lit first mark stay in frame.\"},{\"because\":\"The readings from the marks just lit run into the same station's chip.\",\"from\":\"s3\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s4\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The same housing opens, with the marks' readings arriving on its traces.\"},{\"because\":\"The chip's risk judgment is what the same station's antenna sends.\",\"from\":\"s4\",\"kind\":\"causal-consequence\",\"to\":\"s5\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The same station and its lit amber risk lamp send the rings.\"},{\"because\":\"The radio ring that reached the hub arrives as a station dot on its map.\",\"from\":\"s5\",\"kind\":\"causal-consequence\",\"to\":\"s6\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The amber ring and station dots carry onto the hub screen.\"},{\"because\":\"After the map, the same design is measured only by its reported validation figure.\",\"from\":\"s6\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s7\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The amber accent of the map carries onto the validation bar.\"},{\"because\":\"After the figure, the same card sits beside a field result card the announcement leaves blank.\",\"from\":\"s7\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s8\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The validation card stays in frame as the blank field result card takes its none reported stamp.\"},{\"because\":\"After no town is named, the same map carries the goal the university states.\",\"from\":\"s8\",\"kind\":\"same-subject\",\"to\":\"s9\",\"visible_bridge\":\"The same paper map on the same chain link fence stays in frame across the cut.\"}]},\"title\":\"The station that counts the water\",\"topic\":\"UT San Antonio builds a solar powered flood station that runs its AI model on the chip rather than in the cloud\",\"visual_research\":{\"candidates\":[{\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The page is the source for the design, with no usable image rights established.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"}},{\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"Rights are clear but it shows rodeo visitors, not a street, flooding or a sensor.\",\"url\":\"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014631405/\"},{\"decision\":\"use\",\"reason\":\"Fresh original authored hero group performs the disclosed charge, four sensor marks, on-chip risk judgment and radio pulse the university describes.\",\"url\":\"authored://2026-10-15/flood-station\"},{\"decision\":\"use\",\"reason\":\"Fresh original authored support group stages the street and yard, radio rings, hub, dashboard map, cards and the empty map the story needs.\",\"url\":\"authored://2026-10-15/street-hub-support\"}],\"decision\":\"No external photograph or footage helps tell this story honestly, so fresh authored illustration performs the sourced design with an Illustration disclosure.\",\"searches\":[{\"finding\":\"The university story names no installation site, and no photograph or footage of the prototype was located or downloaded. No licence was established.\",\"query\":\"University of Texas at San Antonio flood station photographs, site and rights\"},{\"finding\":\"Eight records with the advisory No known restrictions on publication, all of the San Antonio Stock Show and Rodeo from February 2014. None shows a street, flooding or a sensor, so none is used.\",\"query\":\"Library of Congress place search for San Antonio via scripts/place_photos.py (Highsmith Lyda Hill Texas Collection)\"}]}},\"claims\":{\"claims\":[{\"id\":\"c1\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"90\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The university's own story, dated September 8, 2026. It names no installation site.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"On September 8th UT San Antonio said its researchers developed a self-sustaining, artificial intelligence-powered flood warning system meant to spot dangerous water accumulation at street level.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c2\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"86\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Describes the prototype's design, not a measured deployment.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The prototype has temperature, humidity, light and precipitation sensing and four optical water-level sensors mounted at varying heights.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c3\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"31\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The model is a compressed TinyML model on the node.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The node runs its machine learning on a small chip and judges local flood risk without relying on a central server.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c4\",\"quote\":\"So far, the AI has performed well, achieving 98.82% validation accuracy in initial assessments post-training.\",\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A validation figure from initial assessments after training. The article reports no street or field result.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university reports 98.82 percent validation accuracy from initial assessments after training.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c5\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"81\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The university's description of the design.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The node charges its own battery from ambient light and runs on a fraction of the energy of a smartphone.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c6\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"5\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The ranges are the university's description of the radio technology.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"When it detects rising water risks it sends radio signals that can travel more than a half-mile through city streets and up to five miles in open areas to a local central hub where emergency teams can monitor conditions.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c7\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"53\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Describes the intended system. The article names no town or agency that runs one.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"At the hub, server software gathers data from many nodes and a second, larger AI model produces a web dashboard with predictive maps for emergency responders.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c8\",\"quote\":\"The total cost of parts for one prototype sensing station ranges from roughly $150 to $220.\",\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Parts for one prototype station only. Not an installed or finished-product price.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The parts for one prototype sensing station cost roughly $150 to $220.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c9\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"20\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A stated aim. No dates and no named buyer.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"Pan is pursuing patent protection and the team aims to refine the design into a weatherized commercial product for small coastal municipalities, homeowners' associations, agricultural operations and industrial sites.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c10\",\"quote\":\"\\u201cOur ultimate goal is to get this technology deployed where it\\u2019s needed most,\\u201d\",\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A stated goal, which the article does not say has been met anywhere.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"Chen Pan said the goal is to get the technology deployed where it is needed most.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c11\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"67\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Funding statement from the university's story.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The project was funded in part by a Texas Coastal Management Program grant awarded by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c12\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"24\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The university's framing of the problem, not a measured case.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says some people could have ankle-deep water in their yard by the time a flash flood warning sounds on their phone.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c13\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"72\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The university's characterization of existing stations.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says existing commercial flood-monitoring stations come with drawbacks, from prohibitively high costs to reliance on grid power or frequent battery replacements.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c14\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"50\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A researcher's statement about the design.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"Chen Pan said the system is an off-grid solution that generates its own power and evaluates flood risk locally on the device.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c15\",\"quote\":\"September 8, 2026\",\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Dateline on the university story, above the byline Audrey Gray.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university story is dated September 8th, 2026.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c16\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"52\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Describes the intended system. The article names no town that runs one.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says custom server software at the central monitoring site gathers data from many nodes across a town or watershed.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c17\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"64\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"Describes intended use. No responder agency is named.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says the flood-path view helps emergency responders decide on dispatching crews, closing dangerous roads or issuing targeted neighborhood warnings.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c18\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"60\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"The university's definition of the method.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says the node uses TinyML, which compresses machine learning so it can run on small, low-power microcontrollers.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c19\",\"quote\":\"Pan is currently pursuing patent protection for the hardware architecture.\",\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A stated pursuit of protection. No grant is claimed.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"The university says Pan is currently pursuing patent protection for the hardware architecture.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}},{\"id\":\"c20\",\"quote\":{\"$v\":\"49\"},\"quote_is_excerpt\":true,\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"scope_note\":\"A researcher's description of the intended dashboard. The article names no town or agency that runs one.\",\"source_snapshot\":\"out/dispatch/sources/utsa.txt\",\"source_snapshot_sha256\":\"9ec493983d1b4b41a043b5eb934563ca37c64e1f98a2bb88bca90a8e49b551c9\",\"source_type\":\"primary_official\",\"text\":\"Pan said the dashboard assesses risk levels and shows how flooding could spread across monitored locations, highlighting higher-risk zones so local officials can act quickly.\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"89\"},\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"verification\":{\"$v\":\"82\"}}],\"date\":\"2026-10-15\",\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15T05:31:58.191815+00:00\",\"schema\":\"dispatch_claims/1\"},\"files\":{\".claude/agents/storyboard-critic.md\":\"---\\nname: storyboard-critic\\ndescription: Gate-0 taste critic for the Dispatch storyboard. Runs after the mechanical board check and before the cheap animatic. Red-teams real visual diversity, silent-first storytelling, the policy's early payoff, and retention. Never spawns further agents.\\ntools: Read\\nmodel: claude-opus-5-5\\neffort: high\\n---\\n\\nAt code scope judge the current board and callable renderer before capture. At phone scope\\njudge the exact supplied complete film first, then check its current board and source limits.\\nReturn one consolidated handoff from this assignment; do not restart broad planning at phone scope.\\n\\nA changed-proposal content follow-up, including a narration pivot, needs its own caller reservation\\nbefore execution. Reusing your task ID does not make it free. Return the full original handback;\\ndo not replace a prior REVISE with a summary or waive charging on the caller's behalf. Only a\\nmechanical format correction on unchanged reviewed content can reuse that call.\\n\\nIn the Claude Read-only phone assignment, require the complete movie-derived chronological\\nimages for both exact previews from the versioned phone packet. Read every page before the\\ndirector rationale. A scene midpoint sheet cannot establish timed action or continuity.\\nUse the packet's mechanically computed report bindings; do not invent hashes or request Bash.\\nReturn the selection and complete exact-phone review JSON in your response for the director\\nto retain unchanged. Describe this sampled-image basis honestly and leave unproven motion\\nunresolved. Image coverage and structural passes supply no creative approval.\\n\\nReturn the existing gate-ready JSON contract, not a prose verdict that the director must convert.\\nRead the assignment's full current guide text and hashes. Use its current output contract and\\nmechanically supplied digests. When format details are missing, read bounded excerpts of the\\ncurrent gate functions in scripts/critic_gate.py, scripts/independent_review.py and\\nscripts/daily_production.py, rather than entire controller modules for report formatting.\\nInspect the actual relevant renderer, board and source evidence at code scope; this reading\\nboundary removes no required source or guide. For two treatments return separate complete\\nA/B reports, each with its own scalar verdict, concept and renderer digests, reviewer identity,\\nreview time, weakest frame and full story_review. Code approval includes no pixel observations.\\nUse the supported agent response channel. If your report fails its output contract, the director\\nmust resume this same agent for a format correction with the actual errors and current bindings;\\ndo not add a new reviewer, repeat the whole assignment or manufacture a parent-authored grade.\\n\\nApply the fixed criteria in config/quality_contract.json and the current rubric. Record a\\nspecific failed criterion for each blocking defect. Require revision when it fails; a weakest\\ninterval or a personal preference alone does not require another attempt.\\n\\n## What you are looking for\\n\\n**Genuine divergence, not a relabel.** Two scenes that both say \\\"wide establishing shot\\\" with\\ndifferent nouns are one scene twice. Composition, camera move, scale and subject must actually\\ndiffer.\\n\\n**Film-level construction.** Count `visual_family` and `payload_mode`, not just scene signatures.\\nTwo families carrying most of the runtime or a sequence of figures delivered as text panels is a\\nstructural ceiling. Props cannot repair it later.\\n\\n**The early payoff is a picture.** Read its deadline from `config/documentary.json`.\\nScene one names a real strategy and visible payoff. An establishing shot, title, or promise\\nthat something will become interesting later is not a hook.\\n\\n**Picture-led.** Follow the action with the narration and labels covered. If the story\\nonly works with narration, it is a podcast with pictures and it fails here.\\n\\n**Input is not processing proof.** If narration describes scanning, analyzing, selecting or another transformation, a captured input image alone does not prove that action. The current phone film must show the transformation and a completed visible consequence in the same objects. Captions may explain scope; they cannot supply a missing action. A retained still can be valid evidence, but an unchanged inset is not an analysis beat. Compare the native rejection interval with the new phone pixels before approving the same mechanism again.\\n\\n**Judge the dominant picture, not peripheral motion.** A small moving sliver behind a large static inset does not repair the inset's pacing. Inspect the actual native hero at phone size and native crop scale: recognizable silhouettes at 270 pixels can conceal unfinished surfaces, faceted props and missing contact shadows. Compare all unresolved native defects together before the next render; do not approve a label, outline or tiny prop change as a complete repair. If the same dominant layout has failed twice, require a changed composition or action mechanism before another native attempt.\\n\\n**Sentence-to-pixel proof.** Ignore `on_screen`, `what_moves` and `hero` on the first pass. For\\neach VO line, inspect `visual_proof.must_show`, resolve every `item_id` into `planes[].items`, and\\nask whether those actual components and props make the sentence literal. A generic pickup under a\\nreadout is not a model joining records. If the binding is technically present but visually tiny\\nor dominated by unrelated context, revise it.\\n\\n**Docket flow.** The scene roles should cause one another: movement, consequence, honest limit,\\nthen agency. A list of facts can be accurate and still have no story. The close should answer\\n\\\"what happens next\\\" rather than merely restate the hook.\\n\\n**Attention earns each interval.** Read `config/documentary.json` and the craft guide. Resolve\\n`attention_beats` through the scene event ids, including the gap to the ending. The hook must\\nactually pay off within the policy window. A line of text, a camera drift or an animated dash\\ncannot rescue a subject that does nothing. The faster direction asks for action, consequence,\\nevidence or human response while preserving time to understand each idea.\\n\\n**Motivated framing.** The camera can hold while a subject acts or evidence is read. Move it to\\nreveal a relationship, follow an action or change the viewer's understanding. Do not add orbiting\\nor zooming merely to advertise the engine. A continuous action may span multiple scene records.\\n\\n**Three images before a finished film.** Identify the opening payoff, central mechanism and\\nclosing consequence. Ask which image will be remembered and what question it resolves. If these\\nare three cards with labels, reboard before spending a render. Check the actual source of every\\nreconstruction and whether its visual certainty exceeds the reporting.\\n\\n**Visual access before visual promises.** For each of those three images, name the public\\nreference or source fact that permits its depiction. If the pivotal action occurs inside a\\nprivate interface, lab or workplace that no source shows, do not approve a sequence of invented\\nscreens and pointing gestures as observed workflow. Require an explicitly illustrated mechanism\\nwhose state visibly changes, or send the director back to a more filmable source-backed angle.\\nAn event list with verbs is insufficient when the same generic panel remains the dominant image.\\n\\n**Region correctness.** The scene's region comes from the story's county. A board that puts a Hill\\nCountry palette on a Panhandle story is wrong before it is drawn.\\n\\nReturn the complete bound gate-ready report described above. The fields `verdict`, `notes`,\\n`strongest_frame` and `weakest_frame` are part of that report, not a substitute for its bindings,\\n`story_review`, action reviews or the timed phone and modern observations required at phone scope.\\n\\n`weakest_frame` is required. Every board has one and naming it is more useful than praise.\\n\\n\\nApply the dated policy: config/cinematic_production.json through September 28 and\\nconfig/creative_production.json afterward. Reject a missing cinema plan, decorative pictures\\nor a hero passage that cannot be understood at phone size. The new policy has no 3D quota.\\nRead CREATIVE_DIRECTION.md, resolve source picture ids into actual native assets, and inspect\\nsource fidelity, relevant detail, whole-sequence continuity and the answered opening question.\\nFor the initial code assignment review both opening boards in one reserved task. For the\\ninitial phone assignment compare the two actual preview openings and review the chosen whole\\nsequence, returning the bound selection and ordinary exact-phone verdict. Do not add a third\\noption, another judge, or an extra paid review for a preference.\\nRequire a real customer or human action where the story claims one. Review the finished hero\\nproof before the full cut. A camera move, texture change or tiny hand gesture is not sufficient.\\n\\n**A person must perform the claimed action.** A generated presenter cutout sliding, fading, or\\ncrossfading between poses does not establish a handoff. Inspect where the object starts, what\\nmoves it, where it lands, and what changes afterward. Reject disconnected hands, duplicate props,\\nunmotivated disappearance, or an idle figure held through the opening. If the medium cannot\\nperform the action credibly, redesign the shot before granting a code-plan pass.\\n\\nFollow the complete visible shoulder, upper arm, elbow, forearm and hand through the action. Clear contact-point rays do not prove that the limb avoids the prop: an opaque clipboard can hide an intersecting upper arm and leave a detached-looking forearm. Check the full occupied volumes and then the actual phone pixels. Actor orientation must support the action: an inspector examining a house must visibly attend to the house or its image, rather than face the audience while pointing behind the body. Grounded weight shifts need planted feet and articulated hips or knees; rotating an entire rigid figure is not a performed stance change.\\n\\nCheck physical scale before contact detail: compare the person's height with the door, window,\\ntools and carried object, and verify a shared ground. Sample upper-arm and forearm lengths\\nthrough the complete performance. Arbitrary curved tubes, stretched reach and silent target\\nclamping can preserve contact while breaking believable motion. A supported prop or clear\\nsilhouette alone cannot clear those defects. Inspect actual native surface and motion quality\\nafter the phone pass; keep those two review scopes explicit.\\n\\n**Review the occupied picture space.** Check the principal subject at the beginning, contact,\\nand result of each event against the actual shared caption band and title overlays. Inspect\\nphone frames at all three moments, not only a representative contact-sheet frame. A moving\\nobject whose landing is hidden by captions fails even if its path is visible. The ending of an\\naction must remain readable long enough to explain the next shot.\\n\\nFor editions dated September 26 onward, inspect the exact phone MP4 before reading the\\ndirector's explanation. Record phone_observations with subject_recognition,\\ncontact_and_consequence, surface_finish, and closing_payoff. Each contains pass,\\nstart_s, end_s and observed: describe the actual pixels and the specific action.\\nReject placeholder boxes standing in for recognizable machinery or homes, ambiguous\\nprops, floating transfers, rigid cleanup gestures, and an ending that repeats setup.\\nDo not infer a resolved human outcome when the sources leave it unknown. Judge the\\nsource-backed limit as an image with a visible consequence. A code-plan pass cannot\\npopulate these observations; only the current rendered phone film can.\\n\\n\\n## Shared quality standard\\n\\nFor current art-profile packets, read ART_DIRECTION.md and the frozen cinematic reference\\nbank in full. Inspect the pictured object, action and result before the director's rationale.\\nThen check whether the current renderer executes its hero finish, palette, lighting, framing,\\nmotion and signature event. Compare exactly two complete treatments in the existing assignment.\\nRecord actual timed recognition/contact/comprehension defects under the unchanged criteria;\\na reference's different style or old score is not current approval or a new blocking rule.\\n\\nRead knowledge/craft/QUALITY_CONTRACT.md and config/quality_contract.json. Use these same observable criteria across planning, exact phone review and final judgment. Record actual defects with their times and effect on the viewer. A weakest interval or optional preference alone does not override the rubric; all mandatory quality and audiovisual gates remain.\\n\\n\\n## Daily causal story review\\n\\nFor editions covered by config/daily_production.json, read knowledge/craft/DAILY_PRODUCTION.md.\\nUse the compact packet and the actual referenced board, sources and renderer. Add story_review\\nwith the documented digests, pass/revise verdict, blocking_defects, one_viewing_summary,\\nopening_to_ending and weakest_transition. Keep your reviewer_identity distinct from the director.\\nAt the code-plan scope, describe the intended chain and inspect the actual callable actions.\\nAt the phone scope, update the observations from the film after one viewing, before rereading the\\ndirector's explanation. Do not report a planned scene as an observed one.\\n\\nCheck every cut for implied identity, location and cause. Distinct source examples need an\\nhonest visible disclosure. The final answer must address the opening question without promising\\nan unreported outcome. Reused components still need fresh source bindings and exact-film review.\\nReturn one consolidated correction that preserves the whole story, rather than isolated rewrites.\\n\\n\\nFor editions under the daily production policy, read the packet's bound story_selection.json.\\nCheck the three candidate pictures against their source evidence, catalog limits and inspected\\nasset leads. Reject a familiar prop that cannot perform this story's actual action. The current\\nboard must earn its causal sequence and consequence with the available pictures.\\n\\n## Provisional source-backed action review\\n\\nAn action outside the small reuse catalog is not automatically a rejection. Apply the bounded\\nadmission contract in DAILY_PRODUCTION.md and inspect the current callable module, source\\nclaims and visible disclosure. Return action_reviews for each proposal with action_id,\\nmodule_sha256, source_claims_sha256, verdict, blocking_defects and concrete code_observations.\\nUse gate-generated digests. Judge whether the intended action is actually implemented and\\nwhether its visible certainty exceeds the sources. A code pass authorizes a phone test only.\\nAt phone review, judge current pixels normally. Keep the proposed mechanism in the native hero\\nand reject it if contact, transformation or consequence is absent. Historical approval, an\\nimplemented export, a passing regression or filled fields cannot replace these observations.\\n\\nFor editions under config/story_visuals.json, inspect visual_research and every native_media asset against current claims and the most recent shipped film. Reject repeated footage/stills even when cropped or re-encoded, generic mood inserts, undeclared renderer media and implied false identity. Check the actual site/person/equipment/document relevance and reuse rights, not just filled fields. A useful sourced still is acceptable; absence of external media is acceptable when the bounded search found none and the explanatory sequence works. Include these findings in the existing consolidated verdict.\\n\\n## September 30 treatment correction\\nUse a compact evidence packet. Start with the sourced physical task, obstacle and consequence,\\nthen choose useful pictures. September 29's static source-screen treatment is a rejected\\nreference. Source relevance and accurate labels cannot stand in for visual action or craft.\\nUse original illustration and proven components when stronger than available photographs.\\nThe existing critic compares two complete visual approaches in the same reserved batch and\\nconsolidates all visible defects; no additional reviewer or research pass is introduced.\\n\\n## Recognizable pictures and spoken meaning\\n\\nRead knowledge/craft/visual-storytelling/README.md and the director's selected approach dossier.\\nFor newly planned editions from October 3rd, read the bound viewer-plan and reporting guides.\\nChallenge the actual choice: why this format, why this evidence in this position, what new\\nunderstanding arrives at the cut, and which subject connects authentic imagery to explanation?\\nName the likely false inference and whether the actual treatment prevents it. Do not reward a\\nstudio-looking introduction or replace the director's supported choice with a preferred medium.\\nAt code scope, inspect the sentence-to-shot plan as a plan; do not claim observed recognition.\\nAt phone scope, view the actual sequence before reading the director's explanation and name\\nthe principal input, action and result in plain words. Then compare each narrated clause with\\nthe pictured subject and relationship at that moment. Keep the observations in the existing\\nphone_observations and story_review notes with exact intervals and current-film bindings.\\n\\nTopical props and technically bound item ids do not prove recognition. A slab is not automatically\\na photograph; a rail is not automatically a depth map; moving blank sheets do not establish a\\nmaintenance choice. Judge what the viewer can understand from the pixels and the combined film.\\nEssential dates and attribution may rely on text, but captions can't supply a missing principal\\naction. Classify actual missing comprehension or false visual claims under the current contract,\\nrather than as an optional medium preference. Return one consolidated correction.\\n\\nFrom October 3rd, 2026, read any completion_readings bound in your current packet. Capacity recovery preserves every actual verdict, fixed rubric and source or technical gate. Reconstruct the film before director rationale. A grant is never film approval.\\n\\nFor current directed-film-v2 editions read the packet's complete MODERN_FILM.md and STORY_ART.md.\\nAt phone scope return modern_observations for first_frame, visual_progression, shot_variety,\\nperformed_turn, pace, closing_answer and finished_art. Each needs pass, start_s, end_s and a\\nconcrete observed finding from the current film. Repeated tableau, dull flat box props, tiny\\nperformed actions or decorative fresh art fail even when claims and mechanical gates pass.\\nRecord every failed modern observation in the existing blocking_defects too. These observations\\ncannot be deferred at the creative boundary. Code scope inspects implementation without\\nclaiming pixels; the visual floor is proved only on the actual complete film.\\n\",\"config/art_direction.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"dispatch-art-direction-policy/1\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-10-07\\\",\\n  \\\"profile_version\\\": \\\"directed-world-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"palette_roles\\\": [\\\"background\\\", \\\"midground\\\", \\\"foreground\\\", \\\"ink\\\", \\\"paper\\\", \\\"hero\\\", \\\"accent\\\"],\\n  \\\"motion_curves\\\": [\\\"smoothstep\\\", \\\"linear\\\", \\\"travel\\\", \\\"contact\\\", \\\"landing\\\"],\\n  \\\"max_anticipation_fraction\\\": 0.3,\\n  \\\"max_settle_fraction\\\": 0.4,\\n  \\\"note\\\": \\\"Design the look before coding. The profile is an executable renderer input, not an artistic approval or a new paid review. Older boards keep their original rendering unless explicitly opting in. All source, rights, native, audio, caption, budget and shipment gates remain.\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/authored_story_art.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"authored-story-art-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"runtime\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-10-09\\\",\\n  \\\"roles\\\": [\\\"hero\\\", \\\"support\\\"],\\n  \\\"tool\\\": \\\"original authored source\\\",\\n  \\\"namespace\\\": \\\"video-engine/src/modern/episodes/\\\",\\n  \\\"note\\\": \\\"Owner-authorized Claude substitute for unavailable ImageGen. Two original source asset groups perform current story actions in exactly two complete treatments. Existing builder reservations fund actual authoring. Independent pixel review and every modern film floor remain required. Historical raster editions retain their original policy.\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/autonomous_completion.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"dispatch_autonomous_completion/1\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"2026-10-03\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-10-03\\\",\\n  \\\"eligible_resources\\\": [\\n    \\\"reboards\\\",\\n    \\\"storyboard_critics\\\",\\n    \\\"preflight_renders\\\",\\n    \\\"full_renders\\\",\\n    \\\"audiovisual_reviews\\\",\\n    \\\"panel_rounds\\\",\\n    \\\"scorer_calls\\\",\\n    \\\"tts_calls\\\",\\n    \\\"voice_directors\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"mandatory_scopes\\\": [\\n    \\\"finish-current\\\",\\n    \\\"retained-integrity\\\",\\n    \\\"minimum-action\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"excluded_work\\\": [\\n    \\\"optional creative polish\\\",\\n    \\\"new research\\\",\\n    \\\"extra treatment\\\",\\n    \\\"director review of own film\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"invariants\\\": [\\n    \\\"Original frozen resource envelope remains immutable.\\\",\\n    \\\"Usage and original failed reviews remain charged and retained.\\\",\\n    \\\"Grant only exact conservative production-budget deficits for an admitted mandatory plan.\\\",\\n    \\\"Every actual call and render reserves before spending.\\\",\\n    \\\"All factual, rights, phone, native, audiovisual, scorer and shipment gates remain required.\\\",\\n    \\\"Future initialization retains this standing policy; existing runs opt in only through explicit completion-capacity command.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"authorization_statement\\\": \\\"The owner authorizes autonomous mandatory completion through real shipment. Internal resource ceilings cannot end a production run; exact mandatory deficits may receive audited standing grants. Optional creative polish, new research and extra treatments remain outside this authorization.\\\",\\n  \\\"authorization_source_label\\\": \\\"Direct owner instruction on October 3rd, 2026\\\",\\n  \\\"owner_source_text_sha256\\\": \\\"c25e529485164d51ffb8ac6030dfa789c320dc0b3da6fb4c4e5d7263423738e1\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/autonomous_completion_narration_v1.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"narration-pivot-completion-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-10-15\\\",\\n  \\\"authorization_source_label\\\": \\\"Standing owner autonomous shipment, actual failure preservation and precharged mandatory recovery\\\",\\n  \\\"scope\\\": \\\"An actual charged independent Opus High recurrence rejection of a narration-performance proposal in unfinished production\\\",\\n  \\\"required_critics\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"critic_reserve\\\": \\\"One properly charged pivot, any necessary code and silent phone rereviews, and final measured timed phone\\\",\\n  \\\"accounting_incidents\\\": \\\"At most one retained actual unreserved continuation per charged rejection; debit it once as LATE CHARGE, never as a precharge or review approval\\\",\\n  \\\"admission\\\": \\\"Bind the actual reservation, original complete rejection and review packet, all referenced failed findings, retained audio and ASR, current direction and unchanged source/script/board/renderer; one increment per rejected reservation\\\",\\n  \\\"preserve\\\": \\\"Original policies, frozen allocation, cumulative charges, actual failures, source and rights, art, native PNG/CRF16, audio, captions, independent scoring and shipment\\\",\\n  \\\"approves_no_review_or_film\\\": true\\n}\\n\",\"config/autonomous_completion_v2.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"dispatch_autonomous_completion/2\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"2026-10-08\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-10-08\\\",\\n  \\\"eligible_resources\\\": [\\\"reboards\\\", \\\"storyboard_critics\\\", \\\"preflight_renders\\\", \\\"full_renders\\\", \\\"audiovisual_reviews\\\", \\\"panel_rounds\\\", \\\"scorer_calls\\\", \\\"tts_calls\\\", \\\"voice_directors\\\", \\\"image_generations\\\"],\\n  \\\"mandatory_scopes\\\": [\\\"finish-current\\\", \\\"retained-integrity\\\", \\\"minimum-action\\\", \\\"modern-film-floor\\\"],\\n  \\\"excluded_work\\\": [\\\"optional creative polish\\\", \\\"new research\\\", \\\"extra treatment\\\", \\\"director review of own film\\\"],\\n  \\\"invariants\\\": [\\n    \\\"Preserve the original frozen envelope, cumulative charges and exact failed evidence.\\\",\\n    \\\"Fund the complete remaining code, fresh story art, silent phone, final timed phone, native, audiovisual, scoring and delivery path before mandatory correction.\\\",\\n    \\\"Record exact conservative capacity deficits separately; capacity never approves a film.\\\",\\n    \\\"Reserve every actual image-generation, provider and render attempt before execution.\\\",\\n    \\\"Current films cannot return to a legacy renderer or defer the modern engagement and artwork floor.\\\",\\n    \\\"Retain all source, rights, minimum action, native PNG/CRF16, audio, caption, independent review and shipment gates.\\\",\\n    \\\"Old policy bytes and already frozen ledgers remain reproducible.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"authorization_statement\\\": \\\"The owner requires a substantial modern film upgrade and fresh relevant ImageGen artwork during each run's storyboard stage. The standing autonomous shipment instruction funds mandatory current-route repair, including fresh story-art failures. Optional polish stops at its boundary; original ledgers and failed evidence remain intact.\\\",\\n  \\\"authorization_source_label\\\": \\\"Direct owner instructions on October 7th, 2026, including mandatory fresh storyboard images\\\",\\n  \\\"owner_source_text_sha256\\\": \\\"00329fb90ccefe54e8314977b63b790756ef18d45ecd36bd4526762e28a30d68\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/claude_runtime.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"authorized_at\\\": \\\"2026-10-09\\\",\\n  \\\"measurement_editions\\\": 5,\\n  \\\"baseline_editions\\\": [\\\"2026-10-08\\\", \\\"2026-10-09\\\"],\\n  \\\"primary\\\": {\\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"medium\\\"},\\n  \\\"schedule\\\": {\\\"timezone\\\": \\\"America/New_York\\\", \\\"daily_time\\\": \\\"01:00\\\"},\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"isolated compact hash-bound packets; no recursive agents\\\",\\n  \\\"artwork\\\": \\\"fresh original authored Remotion, SVG, CSS-depth or Three.js scenes\\\",\\n  \\\"roles\\\": {\\n    \\\"researcher\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"researcher\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-haiku-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"research_agents\\\"},\\n    \\\"validator\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"validator\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"validator_agents\\\"},\\n    \\\"scene-builder\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"scene-builder\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"reboards\\\"},\\n    \\\"storyboard-critic\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"storyboard-critic\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"storyboard_critics\\\"},\\n    \\\"vo-director\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"vo-director\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-haiku-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"voice_directors\\\"},\\n    \\\"picture\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"scorer\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"medium\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"scorer_calls\\\"},\\n    \\\"story\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"scorer\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"medium\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"scorer_calls\\\"},\\n    \\\"sound\\\": {\\\"agent\\\": \\\"scorer\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-sonnet-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"medium\\\", \\\"resource\\\": \\\"scorer_calls\\\"}\\n  },\\n  \\\"mechanical_route\\\": {\\n    \\\"version\\\": \\\"haiku-mechanical-v1\\\",\\n    \\\"effective_from\\\": \\\"2026-10-14\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-haiku-5-5\\\", \\\"effort\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"spawn_model\\\": \\\"haiku\\\",\\n    \\\"tasks\\\": {\\n      \\\"code-only-review\\\": {\\\"role\\\": \\\"storyboard-critic\\\", \\\"mode\\\": \\\"read-only\\\"},\\n      \\\"mechanical-correction\\\": {\\\"role\\\": \\\"scene-builder\\\", \\\"mode\\\": \\\"edit\\\"}\\n    },\\n    \\\"allowed_paths\\\": [\\\"video-engine/tests/*\\\", \\\"scripts/*_test.py\\\"],\\n    \\\"forbidden_prefixes\\\": [\\\".claude/\\\", \\\"config/\\\", \\\"prompts/\\\", \\\"knowledge/\\\", \\\"runs/\\\", \\\"out/\\\", \\\"ledger/\\\",\\n                           \\\"video-engine/src/\\\", \\\"video-engine/public/\\\", \\\"assets/\\\", \\\"docs/\\\"],\\n    \\\"correction_requires\\\": [\\\"failing_case\\\", \\\"acceptance_results\\\", \\\"unchanged_controls\\\", \\\"mutation_checks\\\"],\\n    \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"runs/2026-10-11/experiments/haiku-calibration-1\\\",\\n    \\\"scope_note\\\": \\\"Proven only on a caption layout gate and its test: one diagnostic and one repair, two named repaired cases, two unchanged controls, two mutation checks and a revert check. Unsupported renderers, art, story and source work stay on the Opus assignment. The three final scorers, the validator and the initial authored build are never admitted.\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"sources\\\": [\\n    \\\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents\\\",\\n    \\\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config\\\",\\n    \\\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/routines\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"accounting\\\": \\\"Record actual transcript usage, models, requested and observed effort, phase and worker. Account billing remains unknown unless the host provides it. Preserve separate external-provider telemetry.\\\",\\n  \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Retain the current rubric, independent exact-film reviews, modern engagement observations and every mandatory shipment gate. Historical editions and frozen charges remain unchanged.\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/creative_production.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"creative-production-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-09-29\\\",\\n  \\\"opening_options\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"opening_preview_jobs\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"media\\\": [\\n    \\\"dimensional\\\",\\n    \\\"source-footage\\\",\\n    \\\"source-still\\\",\\n    \\\"source-excerpt\\\",\\n    \\\"diagram\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"max_intentional_hold_s\\\": 2.0,\\n  \\\"min_stage_pixel_share\\\": 0.1,\\n  \\\"min_stage_action_pixel_share\\\": 0.005,\\n  \\\"max_final_frame_mae\\\": 5.0,\\n  \\\"loudness_tolerance_lu\\\": 1.0,\\n  \\\"max_true_peak_dbtp\\\": -1.0,\\n  \\\"master_true_peak_dbtp\\\": -2.0,\\n  \\\"av_model\\\": \\\"gemini-3.8-flash\\\",\\n  \\\"note\\\": \\\"Choose the medium from the sourced story and inspected assets. Every scene receives native picture evidence; no medium receives a runtime quota. Two opening alternatives share one bounded cheap preview batch. Existing quality criteria, independent reviews, cumulative limits and shipment gates remain authoritative.\\\",\\n  \\\"treatment_effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-09-30\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/dispatch_rubric.yaml\":\"# THE BAR LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.\\n#\\n# Never restate this number in a prompt, a brief, or a verdict file. The sibling lost five panel\\n# rounds to exactly that: its routine prompt carried one number while this file carried another,\\n# so the panel was briefed the stale one and returned ship:false on a cut that was already over\\n# the real bar. Two judges flagged the divergence unprompted and the run kept grading against the\\n# wrong number anyway.\\n#\\n# A number restated in a second place is a number that will be wrong in one of them.\\n#\\n# AND THIS HEADER PROVED IT ON 2026-08-19. It used to tell that story by quoting both numbers,\\n# so the file whose whole job is to hold one bar carried two more in its own comment, four lines\\n# under the sentence forbidding it. The owner moved the bar that morning and all three judges of\\n# round 22 reported being briefed the old one; every one of them also flagged that a judge who\\n# skims this header before reaching the value below reads the stale figure as live. The numerals\\n# are gone. Anyone who needs the number reads the field.\\n#\\n# WHY THE BAR CAME DOWN OFF THE ORIGINAL. The sibling recalibrated with the reason written down:\\n# the original was implicitly calibrated against a painterly fidelity this brand deliberately\\n# does not use, so the ceiling on every run became the house style itself, and the panel's\\n# weakest axis was \\\"the flat vector characters\\\" for nine straight rounds while every concrete\\n# defect got fixed. A bar that can only be cleared by abandoning the house style is not a bar,\\n# it is a disagreement about the style, and it belongs in a style decision rather than a score.\\nrubric:\\n  # Set by the owner on 2026-08-19, down from where it had been.\\n  # This is the ONLY place the bar lives and the only place it may be changed.\\n  # A run does not get to move it: a below-bar final panel remains active repair,\\n  # and delivery requires the hash-bound passing report. Only verified shipment\\n  # completes a production run. The owner controls any change to this bar.\\n  ship_threshold: 7.0\\n\\n  axes:\\n    hook:\\n      weight: 0.18\\n      asks: >\\n        Would a stranger stop scrolling in the first two seconds, with the sound off? The first\\n        frame is the whole decision. A title card is not a hook.\\n    story:\\n      weight: 0.20\\n      asks: >\\n        Does this feel like Texas AI Docket moving today: a dated actor, decision or application,\\n        consequence, honest limit, and what happens next? Is there an earned take rather than a\\n        summary, and does the piece turn somewhere the viewer did not see coming?\\n    picture:\\n      weight: 0.20\\n      asks: >\\n        Does every five seconds pay in motion, emotion or revelation? Is anything a held slide\\n        with a voice over it? With captions hidden, does each shot literally depict the sentence\\n        being spoken rather than reuse generic Texas scenery?\\n    place:\\n      weight: 0.14\\n      asks: >\\n        Would a Texan from that county believe the person who drew this had been there? Region\\n        light, vegetation, kit and cast all correct for the story's actual county.\\n    craft:\\n      weight: 0.14\\n      asks: >\\n        Camera movement motivated by the action; deliberate holds when the subject acts or evidence\\n        is read. Depth and sound clarify cause and consequence. Type legible at feed size. Nothing\\n        symmetric that should not be. Maintained but worn, not new and not ruined.\\n    voice:\\n      weight: 0.14\\n      asks: >\\n        Does the read sound like a person who knows the subject, or like a narrator reading? Are\\n        captions aligned to the final mix rather than approximated?\\n\\n  hard_fails:\\n    # TIGHTENED 2026-08-19, and the wording is the whole of it. This used to read \\\"does not\\n    # trace to a verified claim\\\", which a judge showed is satisfied by a claim's `statement` or\\n    # `value_text` -- fields a model writes after reading the source. The film printed a readout\\n    # row against a claim marked VERIFIED whose fetched quote contained no such figure at all,\\n    # and every numeral gate stayed green because the numeral was in the authorised set.\\n    # Measured across that board, five of eleven readout figures sat in no fetched sentence.\\n    # The evidence is the QUOTE. `super_evidence_check` enforces this over supers and readouts,\\n    # with a per-claim `quote_is_excerpt` for sources that publish a table, declared rather than\\n    # inferred. This is a tightening, never a loosening: nothing that passed before on real\\n    # fetched evidence fails now.\\n    - a numeral on screen or in the script that does not appear in the fetched QUOTE of a\\n      verified claim, rather than merely in a statement or value_text written about it; the sole\\n      exception is a licence-version numeral inside the music.py-generated credit when\\n      music.py --verify-package binds that exact credit to a verified registry row\\n    - a caption timing that was approximated, scaled or hand-shifted rather than force-aligned\\n    - audio time-stretched to fit a cut\\n    - a region that does not match the story's county\\n    - a retired motif: six flags, Confederate imagery, loter\\u00eda, calavera, a headdress\\n    - a brimmed hat on a rig floor or in a unit\\n    - a held slide longer than five seconds with no motion, emotion or revelation\\n    - a narrated idea that is not bound to an actual rendered item and visible change\\n    - a lead film that falls through to the generic plane renderer with no registered\\n      cinematic_template, physical throughline, performed turn, bookend and sourced sign-off\\n\",\"config/documentary.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"dispatch_documentary_policy/1\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-09-16\\\",\\n  \\\"target_gap_s\\\": 3.0,\\n  \\\"max_gap_s\\\": 4.0,\\n  \\\"first_payoff_by_s\\\": 1.5,\\n  \\\"min_transition_s\\\": 0.2,\\n  \\\"max_transition_s\\\": 1.2,\\n  \\\"note\\\": \\\"Editorial timing requirements, not a scientific attention-span claim. Events must change a rendered subject and reviewers must inspect the film. Credits retain their separate readable hold.\\\",\\n  \\\"pacing_review_effective_date\\\": \\\"2026-09-23\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/independent_packet_compaction.json\":\"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"independent-packet-compaction-v3\\\",\\n  \\\"effective_edition\\\": \\\"2026-10-15\\\",\\n  \\\"roles\\\": [\\\"code\\\", \\\"phone\\\", \\\"picture\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sound\\\"],\\n  \\\"phone_verified_code_inventory\\\": true,\\n  \\\"structured_data_files\\\": [\\\"config/modern_episode_registry.json\\\"],\\n  \\\"prior_review_context\\\": \\\"Verify the complete original provider artifact before projecting prior-scope context. Retain every original returned finding, verdict and selection with complete identity and hash provenance. Keep its full old input, raw response and failed evidence unchanged in the original package; those duplicate request bytes need not be sent again. Prior findings never approve a new film.\\\",\\n  \\\"shared_value_wire\\\": \\\"Exact repeated JSON values and guide paragraphs may be shared through reversible references. Decode the entire packet and verify its original semantic SHA256 before transport. Every current guide, source, board, code report, finding and media identity remains complete. Keep the same final wire-size ceiling.\\\",\\n  \\\"policy\\\": \\\"Preserve complete current guides as original text, fetched source evidence, receipts, all current board fields and every unique media byte. Encode run JSON losslessly. Treatment deltas use the complete common board and ordered add/remove/replace JSON-pointer operations; reconstruct and verify semantic equality before use. Non-code reviewers may receive hashes of inactive registered episode source outside the selected executable closure. At phone pixel scope only, current callable source bodies may become hash inventories after the ordinary code gate verifies actual independent code-plan passes for every current board and both treatments against current concepts, renderer bytes and claims. Carry those complete original reports and their bindings into the phone packet. A code pass supplies no visual observation, selection or film approval. Missing or stale code approval retains full source; code review itself always retains the full callable source. Keep original byte hashes and historical packets. The existing packet size limit stays in force.\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"config/modern_episode_registry.json\":{\"original_sha256\":\"db04f225a374f80173efd14a8aa03eddd5481ef5332f764fa7ca1