WebFetch refused a verbatim reproduction, so I checked each quote one by one. I couldn't read the GLO Final Report E014 PDF, which is over the fetch size limit. That no longer affects the result, because s13 is now limited to the announcement. I didn't see the on-screen cards or tags, the board, the audio take, the captions or the 9.5 second scene timing, and this review approves none of them.\",\"rulings\":{\"announcement_wording_ruling\":\"PASS. 'The announcement reports no field result.' is accepted in place of 'The university's announcement reports no field result.' It is not a work-around of my R9 requirement, because it fixes the actual defect, which was a negative claim scoped to the institution.\",\"question_1_s11_s12\":\"My R9 ruling stands unchanged: supported. s11 and s12 keep the university attribution, the chip AI subject, the validation metric and the early or initial timing. 'post-training' isn't spoken. That doesn't make the claim stronger, and s13 rules out any field reading.\",\"question_2_s13_s14\":\"'The announcement' is limited enough to the source, and I don't require my exact wording. My R9 objection was that 'The university' widened an absence found in one document to the whole institution. 'The announcement' makes the absence a property of a single document, and that document is the one verified: the live page and the snapshot report no field result and name no installed site. In the spoken sequence the only announcement in view is the university's. s2 says 'UT San Antonio says' and s11 says 'The university reports', so the listener can't take it to mean a different or wider body of work. 'university's' would only repeat who issued it, which is already established. s14's 'It' now refers to the announcement and is supported as written. PASS on both sentences.\"},\"script_sha256\":\"67d7ae453299483c5539290989560cdffa5ce4c6ad75fdfaef5929793d7feadf\",\"sentences\":[{\"claim_ids\":[\"c12\"],\"limits_note\":\"Source has 'some people could have ank