al optical sensor pod and subsequent flooded yard scenes are clearly identifiable, though the opening frame is purely technical.\",\"pass\":true,\"start_s\":0.0},\"surface_finish\":{\"end_s\":15.62,\"observed\":\"Surfaces feature clean metallic gradients, well-defined shadows, and crisp line weights suited to mobile screen playback.\",\"pass\":true,\"start_s\":8.68}},\"retained_findings_review\":[{\"finding\":\"Prior host media review reported partial probe failure with [media removed: request limit].\",\"reason\":\"Direct frame inspection of Treatment B's complete rendered MP4 video artifact verifies full visual rendering across all scenes without anomalies.\",\"status\":\"resolved_by_full_context\"}],\"story_review\":{\"blocking_defects\":[],\"one_viewing_summary\":\"Beginning on an optical sensor mark being submerged by floodwater, the camera cuts to a flooded San Antonio yard where a smartphone receives an alert after water has already gathered. The video then introduces the prototype station, demonstrating its solar self-charging mechanism and lack of cell tower reliance. Water ascends the post, tripping four optical sensors at staggered heights alongside weather indicators. An internal TinyML processor analyzes conditions on-device, registering flood risk without remote server processing. The station broadcasts a radio signal over a half-mile in city streets and up to five miles in open land to a hub, where a second model renders predictive flood paths for responders. The report clarifies that while the system demonstrated 98.82% validation accuracy post-training, no field deployments exist, closing with the deployment plan stowed in the prototype's travel case.\",\"opening_to_ending\":\"Traces the complete narrative arc from sensor submergence and delayed phone warning to autonomous edge AI computation, radio hub mapping, lab accuracy disclosures, and pending deployment.\",\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"weakest_transition\":\"The match cut at 00:01.955 from the isola