were not located or downloaded.\",\"rights_basis\":\"None established. No reuse licence found, so no photograph is admitted.\",\"source_url\":{\"$v\":\"344\"},\"url\":{\"$v\":\"344\"},\"what_it_proves\":\"Nothing the film needs. The article is the source for the design.\"}],\"human_consequence\":\"Emergency responders and residents of street-level flood-prone blocks. No affected resident or responder is named in any source.\",\"mechanism\":\"Input: four optical water-level sensors plus temperature, humidity, light and rain sensing. Transformation: a compressed model on the chip judges flood risk locally. Result: a radio warning goes to a hub and a dashboard of predicted flood paths. Limit: validation accuracy only and no named installation.\",\"opening_action\":\"An illustrated solar station beside a street. Water rises past four sensor marks at different heights. Disclosed as Illustration.\",\"source_limit\":\"The design and accuracy claims rest on the university's own account. The accuracy figure is from initial assessments after training. No installed site, no field result and no price for a finished product are published.\",\"three_key_images\":[\"The solar station with four sensor heights, drawn as an illustration\",\"Water crossing the four marks one at a time while the chip lights a local risk judgment\",\"A radio pulse to a hub and a dashboard of predicted flood paths beside the card that says no installed site is named\"],\"viewer_question\":{\"$v\":\"341\"}},\"movement\":{\"action\":\"published a field-ready prototype flood warning node that runs its machine learning model on the chip\",\"actor\":\"University of Texas at San Antonio\",\"date\":\"2026-09-08\",\"event_type\":\"published\",\"object\":\"a solar powered street-level station with four optical water-level sensors at different heights\"},\"record_id\":\"tx-2026-0141\",\"record_status\":\"in-record\",\"record_url\":\"https://texasaidocket.com/item/tx-2026-0141/\",\"sources\":[{\"note\":\"UT San Antonio Today, September 8th, 2026, Audrey Gray\",\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15\",\"source_type\":\"primary\",\"url\":{\"$v\":\"344\"}},{\"note\":\"Texas AI Docket record tx-2026-0141 with ten verified quote claims\",\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15\",\"source_type\":\"primary\",\"url\":\"https://texasaidocket.com/item/tx-2026-0141/\"},{\"note\":\"Texas General Land Office Coastal Management Program project page, started December 1st, 2023\",\"retrieved\":\"2026-10-15\",\"source_type\":\"primary\",\"url\":\"https://glo.texas.gov/es/node/100126\"}],\"texas_link\":\"UT San Antonio researchers in Bexar County built a prototype street-level flood warning station. The announcement names no installation site, so the film draws San Antonio streets as an illustration and says prototype.\",\"title\":\"The station that counts the water\",\"visual_stakes\":{\"affected_group\":\"Emergency responders and residents on street-level flood-prone blocks in Texas towns and cities.\",\"affected_person\":\"An illustrated emergency worker watching a dashboard and a driver on a street-level block that floods first, both disclosed illustration\",\"answer\":\"The design says yes, on its own solar power. The proof so far is validation accuracy, not a street.\",\"asset_fit\":\"Two fresh authored source groups, a flood station hero and a street and hub support group, shared by two complete treatments. Diagram and illustration only, no footage and no real site.\",\"consequence\":\"A dashboard of predicted flood paths for emergency responders, per the university's description. No place is named as using it.\",\"opening_question\":{\"$v\":\"341\"},\"physical_subject\":\"a solar flood station with four water-level sensors at different heights\",\"source_urls\":[{\"$v\":\"344\"}],\"visible_change\":\"water crosses the four sensor marks in turn, the chip judges the risk, and a radio pulse leaves for a hub\"},\"why_today\":\"UT San Antonio published the prototype on September 8th, 2026. The Docket carries it as 